      "name": "Antigravity",
      "defaultModel": "gemini-4-hydrogen",
      "source": "Fleet agy ACP (agy 1.5.9, 2026-11-16): these models are served by the gateway's Messages-to-agy-ACP bridge (`agy acp`, subscription sign-in), not by a direct Cloud Code Assist chat call. `agy acp` reports availableModels as rung ids (`gemini-4-argon-medium`, `claude-opus-5-5-high`) and `configOptions.model` as the base id (`gemini-4-argon`, `claude-opus-5-5`) with the rung in a separate `thought_level` (low/medium/high); a model the account's availableModels lacks fails session open. Underlying Gemini models are 1M (`usage_update` size 1,048,576), but agy checkpoints Gemini sessions around ~511k-514k tokens (observed 2026-11-16 on gemini-4-hydrogen), so all Antigravity entries (Gemini and Claude) declare a fixed 500,000-token Fleet window (matching Codex GPT: a Fleet-defined window rather than the model's own), and their Claude Code discovery ids still carry the `[1m]` marker so the child does not clamp them to 200k — the auto-compact point is therefore 500,000 − 16,000 = 484,000. Quota is read from agy -p /usage (agy 1.1.11 or newer): the Gemini models spend the gemini-weekly and gemini-5h pools (Argon also has gemini-argon-wtu-weekly), and the Claude models spend the separate 3p-weekly and 3p-5h pools — the two families never share headroom (measured 2026-11-16). `gpt-oss-120b-medium` sits in the same agy group but is deliberately not served here, so it appears in no catalog, discovery, roster or picker. Earlier measurements (agy 1.1.18-1.4.1, Cloud Code Assist fetchAvailableModels/streamGenerateContent, 2026-09-03 to 2026-11-13) confirmed the Gemini 3.8 Flash, Gemini 4 Argon, Carbon and Hydrogen rungs; Hydrogen also lists xhigh.",
